{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "medium", "id": 389, "key": "hp_laptop_not_thermally_dead", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "clocks held 2.4-2.7 GHz (above the 1.8 GHz base) and rose through the run, zero new throttle events", "review_after": null, "source_id": null, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "hardware", "validity": "SUSPECT", "validity_reason": "Legacy fact has no source or quote; recover the original evidence before relying on it", "value": "CONTRADICTION. Sprint handoff 01 (2026-09-07) asserts 'The HP laptop's fans are dead' and that it was 'thermally throttled to about 1.4 GHz for the whole of the second campaign'. The memory note fan-90b-diagnosis (measured 2026-09-09 and 2026-09-11) rules out mechanical failure: 61s of 8-thread load held 2.4-2.7 GHz (ABOVE the 1.8 GHz base), peaked 82C with zero new throttle events, and over 3h of ordinary use throttling cost 12.1s = 0.11% of wall time. The 90B error is most likely a tachometer/EC reporting fault. CAVEAT: a 61-second test does not establish multi-HOUR sustained training throughput, so this does not prove the HP can train for hours - it only falsifies 'dead fan / 1.4 GHz always'."}
