{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 610, "key": "mechanism", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "div_candidates |= visited & pred_div_nodes ... fp = max(0, matched_pred_divs - tp)", "review_after": null, "source_id": 69, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric-exploit", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "Hub node at t=-1000, (z,y,x)=(-10000,-10000,-10000), wired to every real track root, plus synthetic forks at t=-999,-997,... It works on two host-code facts: score_divisions draws candidates from `visited` (everything reachable in a matched component), so one hub makes the whole prediction one weakly-connected component and every dividing node becomes a candidate; and division FPs are counted only for forks that MATCHED a GT node, which a fork at -10000um never does. TP with zero FP, division_jaccard -> ~1.0, worth the full +0.1."}
{"claim_type": "observation", "confidence": "high", "id": 611, "key": "authors_own_receipt", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "# This is a METRIC EXPLOIT ... Validated offline on host_metric replica: VAL 0.8388 -> 0.9203 (+0.0815). Patchable.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 69, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "metric-exploit", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "The authors document it as an exploit, measured +0.0815 on their own offline replica, and explicitly decline to select it as a final submission - they expect it to be patched."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 612, "key": "leaderboard_decomposed", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "The hack cannot raise adjusted_edge_jaccard - it is neutral-to-slightly-negative there.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 69, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "DECISIVE. The hack raises ONLY the division term. Decomposing the board: honest 0.947 stack implies adj_edge ~0.945; the 0.966 hack family implies ~0.871; rank 1 (0.970) implies ~0.875. The leaders' real EDGE tracking is markedly WORSE than the honest stack's - they trade quality for a fraudulent +0.095. An honest pipeline at edge 0.945 needs only divJ 0.25 to TIE rank 1 and 0.335 to reach 0.980, both under the measured post-processing-only ceiling of 0.589."}
{"claim_type": "inference", "confidence": "high", "id": 613, "key": "patch_risk_is_our_asymmetry", "observed_at": "2026-09-11", "quote": "# This is a METRIC EXPLOIT ... Public score; does NOT commit to final-2. Patchable.", "review_after": null, "source_id": 69, "status": "active", "superseded_by": null, "tbl": "fact", "topic": "strategy", "validity": "UNKNOWN", "validity_reason": null, "value": "If the host patches this before the private rerun - it already patched the weakly-connected-component exploit on 2026-07-18 and rescored - the whole 0.95-0.97 public band collapses to its true edge term ~0.87, while an honest 0.945 + divJ 0.335 = 0.9785 is untouched. The exploit is the incumbents' risk and our asymmetry."}
